{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>You shall not test YHWH your Elohim</b> — the meaning is: as you tested at Massah, that you should not say, \"If HaShem is in our midst to perform miracles for us, or whether we shall succeed in serving before Him and be sated with bread and fare well — then we will keep His Torah.\" For that was precisely the intention there: that if they saw that HaShem would give them water by miracle from Him, they would follow Him in the wilderness, and if not, they would abandon Him. And it was reckoned to them as a great sin, because after it had been verified to them by signs and wonders that Moses is the prophet of HaShem and the word of YHWH in his mouth is truth, it is not fitting to do anything further as a test, and whoever does so is not testing the prophet but rather testing HaShem blessed be He, to know whether the hand of YHWH is shortened. Therefore He forbade for all generations to test the Torah or the prophets, for it is not fitting to serve HaShem by way of doubt or by requesting a sign or a test, for it is not the will of HaShem to perform miracles for every person and at all times, and it is not fitting to serve Him on condition of receiving a reward — perhaps one may even find in His service and in walking in the ways of the Torah pain and harm, and it is fitting to accept everything in just judgment, and not as the fools of our nation said (Malachi 3:14), \"It is vain to serve Elohim; and what profit is it that we have kept His charge?\" And as it says there (Malachi 3:14), \"And we walk mournfully because of HaShem.\" Therefore Scripture says here, <b>You shall diligently keep His commandments and His testimonies</b> — these are the miracles He did for you in the past, to serve as a testimony for you, such as Pesah, Matzah, and Sukkah — <b>and His statutes</b>, even though you do not know their reason, for in truth it will be well for you in the end; there is no need for testing in the Torah and the commandments after it has already been verified to you that it is from Him blessed be He. And likewise regarding any prophet who has been tested and established as a true prophet by signs and wonders — the law is the same as that of the Torah: you shall not test his words with any reward or any punishment he may tell you, and you shall not doubt His exalted ability, but believe in His Torah and trust in His prophets and you will prosper. Now He promised that the culmination of glory will come with the inheritance of the Land and the defeat of the enemies, for that is the great good and the necessity for that generation. And afterward He said that for the coming generations as well there is no need for testing in the performance of the commandments, but rather they should ask their fathers and their elders, and they will tell them the truth of the Torah and the commandments, as He will explain (Deuteronomy 6:20), <b>When your son asks you</b>, and the rest of the passage."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The word קְדֹרַנִּית in the Malachi quotation is rendered hyper-literally as 'mournfully' following its context in Malachi 3:14; the Hebrew term is unusual and its precise sense debated."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:46:30.832728597Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}